=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Router6 has a misconfigured iptables FORWARD chain that drops virtually all transit traffic arriving on its eth2 interface (the r6_r3 uplink connecting it to the rest of the network via router3).

The chain contains four rules keyed on `in eth2`:
1. ACCEPT TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 → 172.18.51.1 (a single narrow HTTP allow)
2. DROP all TCP from eth2
3. DROP all UDP from eth2
4. DROP all remaining protocols (proto 0 / "all") from eth2

This blocks every protocol — including ICMP — on the WAN-facing interface in the forwarding path. The hit counters confirm it: 8 ICMP packets (8 × 84 bytes = exactly ping-sized) caught by rule 4, and 25 UDP packets caught by rule 3.

The failure is bidirectional because both traffic flows transit eth2. Forward path (PC4 → PC15): packet enters router6 on eth2, rules 2–4 drop it. Return path (PC15 → PC4): PC15's reply exits router6 on eth2 fine, but PC4's ICMP echo-reply re-enters router6 on eth2 on the return leg and is dropped. Confirmed by traceroute from PC4 stopping cold after hop 5 (router6), and by PC15 being unable to ping even router3 (10.50.1.1) — its reply transits eth2 and gets dropped.

Router6 itself can originate and receive traffic fine (INPUT/OUTPUT chains are unaffected), which is why direct pings from router6 to PC4 and PC15 both succeed.

Root cause: incorrectly broad DROP rules were added to router6's FORWARD chain on eth2, intended perhaps as a firewall policy but instead blocking all inter-site forwarding between Location C (172.18.50/51) and everywhere else.

Corrective fix: on router6, flush the FORWARD chain (`iptables -F FORWARD`) and replace it with a properly scoped policy — or at minimum remove rules 2–4 and replace them with stateful rules (`-m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT`) so that legitimate return traffic is not silently dropped.
=== END ===